Notes for CaitSith project

CaitSith (which was derived from TOMOYO Linux 1.8.3) is an access restriction
module for Linux systems. This module gives you ability to restrict access
(e.g. opening files, executing programs) at the kernel level.

This module is released under the GPLv2.

Project URL: https://caitsith.sourceforge.net/

ChangeLog:

Version 0.1   2012/04/01   First release.

  ccs-patch-\*.diff for TOMOYO 1.8.x is reusable to CaitSith by converting

    sed -e 's/CCSECURITY/CAITSITH/g' -e 's/ccsecurity/caitsith/g'

  before feeding to patch command.

Fix 2012/05/04

    @ Fix compiler warning.

      Some compilers complained possible use of uninitialized variable
      in ccs_read_policy(), though it actually should not happen.

    @ Do not continue if policy version was not specified by policy file.

      In order to stop if policy file was by error empty,
      set default policy version to undefined.

Version 0.1.1   2012/05/05   Bug fix release.

Fix 2012/07/12

    @ Fix wrong operation name when selectively reading via query id.

      Operation name "execute" was by error always printed when
      reading via query id.

    @ Fix garbage collection check.

      If an acl block was deleted without deleting decision lines in the acl
      block, garbage collector was by error unable to reclaim memory.

Version 0.1.2   2012/07/12   Bug fix release.

Fix 2012/08/02

    @ Fix identical conditions detection under 64 bits environment.

      Detecting identical conditions was randomly failing because memory block
      which holds conditions part by error had uninitialized 32 bits of hole
      under 64 bits environment.

Version 0.1.3   2012/08/02   Bug fix release.

Fix 2012/08/17

    @ Fix kfree() oops.

      If CONFIG_CAITSITH_USE_EXTERNAL_TASK_SECURITY is not set, and no process
      is using statically assigned ccs_kernel_domain object when
      /proc/caitsith/ interface is close()d, the object was by error subjected
      to kfree() by the garbage collector.

Version 0.1.4   2012/08/17   Bug fix release.

Fix 2012/08/31

    @ Fix environment variable checking logic.

      envp["ENVNAME"] was returning incorrect comparison results. However,
      envp["ENVNAME"] still returns (you might think) incorrect comparison
      results if multiple values with the same environment variable name
      (e.g. HOME=/ and HOME=/root ) are passed in a way comparison results
      differ. For further restriction, you might also want to use
      "acl environ" in addition to "acl execute".

Version 0.1.5   2012/08/31   Bug fix release.

Fix 2012/10/08

    @ Fix KABI breakage on Ubuntu 12.10.

      I was using include/linux/security.h as the common path for pulling in
      include/linux/caitsith.h so that I can avoid scattering #include line.

      When scripts/genksyms/genksyms calculates hash values for Module.symvers
      file, it uses the extracted form of involved structures if the structure
      layout is known but it instead uses UNKNOWN if the structure layout is
      not known. Therefore, pulling in include files that define structure's
      layout from include/linux/caitsith.h causes changes in the hash values
      and causes KABI breakage, even if no changes were made to the involved
      structures.

      Fix this breakage by avoiding pulling in include/linux/sched.h and
      include/linux/dcache.h from include/linux/caitsith.h where possible.

Fix 2012/11/01

    @ Fix ccs_manager() oops.

      If CONFIG_CAITSITH_USE_EXTERNAL_TASK_SECURITY is not set and
      current->ccs_domain_info is not yet assigned, ccs_manager() triggered
      oops when printing denial message.

Version 0.1.6   2012/11/01   Bug fix release.

    @ Allow more flexible conditions.

      Updated policy parser in order to allow more flexible conditions.
      As a side effect, information available in the audit logs may have
      changed.

Version 0.1.7   2012/12/25   Functionality enhancement release.

    @ Fix policy parser regressions.

      Parsing errors in "environ" and "pivot_root" are fixed.

Version 0.1.8   2013/01/06   Bug fix release.

    @ Support multiple concurrent LKM-based LSM modules.

      Commit a2a8474c "exec: do not sleep in TASK_TRACED under
      ->cred_guard_mutex" moved "current->in_execve = 1;" from before
      prepare_bprm_creds() to after prepare_bprm_creds(). It turned out that,
      as an unexpected bonus, we can use security_prepare_creds() as a hook for
      emulating security_bprm_free() hook.

      I updated the logic for security_bprm_free() emulation, and now LKM-based
      LSM version of CaitSith should be able to coexist with other AKARI-like
      LKM-based LSM implementations on all kernel versions other than 2.6.29
      and 2.6.30.

    @ Fix policy parser regressions.

      Parsing errors in "handler" and "transition" are fixed.

Version 0.1.9   2013/02/14   Functionality enhancement release.

    @ Fix incompatible argument warning.

      The hook for checking chown/chgrp permission was by error using
      uid_t/gid_t instead of kuid_t/kgid_t, resulting in compiler warning with
      3.5 and later kernels.

    @ Change symbol prefix.

      ccs_ prefix was changed to cs_ .
      CCS_loader= and CCS_trigger= were changed to CS_loader= and CS_trigger= .

      ccs-patch-\*.diff for TOMOYO 1.8.x is reusable to CaitSith by converting

        sed -e 's/CCSECURITY/CAITSITH/g' -e 's/ccsecurity/caitsith/g' -e 's/ccs_domain_info/cs_domain_info/g' -e 's/ccs_flags/cs_flags/g'

      before feeding to patch command.

Version 0.1.10   2013/05/12   Bug fix release.

Fix 2015/01/01

    @ Fix missing chmod(-1) check in Linux 3.1 and later kernels.

      Commit e57712ebebbb9db7 "merge fchmod() and fchmodat() guts, kill
      ancient broken kludge" changed chmod(-1) from no-op to setting to
      07777. Therefore, CaitSith must not ignore chmod(-1) case.

    @ Fix potentially using bogus attributes when stat() fails.

      We should reset attributes information when executing handler program,
      or attributes of original program could be used when stat() on handler
      program failed.

Version 0.1.11   2015/01/12   Bug fix release.

Fix 2015/04/08

    @ Fix incorrect readdir() permission check.

      CONFIG_CAITSITH_READDIR was meant for allowing users to control readdir()
      permission check. However, CONFIG_CAITSITH_GETATTR was by error used for
      controlling readdir() permission check. This fix should not affect
      kernels built with default configuration, for both
      CONFIG_CAITSITH_READDIR and CONFIG_CAITSITH_GETATTR are defined by
      default.

Version 0.1.12   2015/04/08   Bug fix release.

Fix 2015/06/04

    @ Fix incorrect open() argument.

      The first argument for cs_open_permission() in LKM-based LSM version
      for RHEL 6 branch was wrong since 0.1.11.

Version 0.1.13   2015/06/06   Bug fix release.

Fix 2015/07/19

    @ Fix truncated quota reporting.

      The audit[$index] quota lines were reported for only 0 <= $index <= 2
      by error. Now, all 0 <= $index <= 255 will be reported.

Version 0.1.14   2015/07/21   Bug fix release.

Fix 2015/08/03

    @ Limit wildcard recursion depth.

      Since wildcards that need recursion consume kernel stack memory,
      we cannot allow infinite recursion.

Fix 2015/08/04

    @ Fix incorrect /\(dir\)/ pattern match.

      "/\(\*\)/\*" was failing to match "/" because "/\(\*\)/" did not match
      "/" due to a bug in pattern matching function.

Version 0.1.15   2015/08/06   Bug fix release.

Fix 2016/06/07

    @ Fix pathname calculation on overlayfs.

      Since Linux 3.17, cs_realpath() was not calling cs_get_local_path()
      when cs_get_absolute_path() returned -EINVAL. As a result, larger
      memory allocation was needlessly attempted and access was finally
      rejected due to out of memory error when used on overlayfs.

Version 0.1.16   2016/06/07   Bug fix release.

Fix 2016/09/25

    @ Fix memory usage accounting for conditions.

      cs_get_condition() forgot to resize conditional part before adding to
      list. As a result, each conditional part consumed PAGE_SIZE bytes
      rather than entry->size bytes, which can far exceed memory quota for
      policy.

Fix 2016/09/26

    @ Use ksize() for more accurate memory usage accounting.

      cs_round2() is not always accurate because available slab objects
      might be version/config dependent.

Version 0.1.17   2016/09/28   Bug fix release.

Fix 2016/10/03

    @ Use /sys/kernel/security/caitsith/ rather than /proc/caitsith/ .

      In preparation for proposing CaitSith for mainline, I changed policy
      interface.

Version 0.2   2016/10/05   Policy interface update release.

Fix 2017/02/02

    @ Use for_each_thread() for GC operation.

      while_each_thread() without tasklist_lock is not safe.
      Use for_each_process_thread() if it is available, hold
      tasklist_lock otherwise.

Version 0.2.1   2017/02/20   Bug fix release.

Fix 2017/08/18

    @ Use smp_rmb() when waiting for hook readiness.

      "while (!cond);" is implicitly optimized like "if (!cond) while (1);".
      Use "while (!cond) smp_rmb();" in order to prevent such optimization.

Version 0.2.2   2017/09/17   Bug fix release.

Fix 2018/03/26

    @ Fix wrong probe_kernel_write() usage.

      Previously registered LSM hook (if any) was by error overwritten
      by this hook. This bug affects only LKM-based LSM version of CaitSith
      for 4.12 and later kernels.

Version 0.2.3   2018/04/01   Bug fix release.

Fix 2019/07/27

    @ Change pathname calculation for read-only filesystems.

      Commit 5625f2e3266319fd ("TOMOYO: Change pathname for non-rename()able
      filesystems.") intended to be applied to filesystems where the content is
      not controllable from the userspace (e.g. proc, sysfs, securityfs), based
      on an assumption that such filesystems do not support rename() operation.

      But it turned out that read-only filesystems also do not support rename()
      operation despite the content is controllable from the userspace, and that
      commit is annoying TOMOYO users who want to use e.g. squashfs as the root
      filesystem due to use of local name which does not start with '/'.

      Therefore, based on an assumption that filesystems which require the
      device argument upon mount() request is an indication that the content
      is controllable from the userspace, do not use local name if a filesystem
      does not support rename() operation but requires the device argument upon
      mount() request.

    @ Reject move_mount() system call for now.

      Commit 2db154b3ea8e14b0 ("vfs: syscall: Add move_mount(2) to move mounts
      around") introduced security_move_mount() LSM hook, but we missed that
      TOMOYO and AppArmor did not implement hooks for checking move_mount(2).
      Since unchecked mount manipulation is not acceptable, for now pretend
      as if move_mount(2) is unavailable.

    @ Don't check open/getattr permission on sockets.

      syzbot found that use of SOCKET_I()->sk from open() can result in
      use after free problem, for socket's inode is still reachable via
      /proc/pid/fd/n despite destruction of SOCKET_I()->sk already completed.

      But there is no point with calling security_file_open() on sockets
      because open("/proc/pid/fd/n", !O_PATH) on sockets fails with -ENXIO.

      There is some point with calling security_inode_getattr() on sockets
      because stat("/proc/pid/fd/n") and fstat(open("/proc/pid/fd/n", O_PATH))
      are valid. But since information which can be protected by checking
      security_inode_getattr() on sockets is trivial, let's not check it.

Version 0.2.4   2019/08/20   Bug fix release.

Fix 2019/12/07

    @ Don't use nifty names on sockets.

      Revert "Don't check open/getattr permission on sockets.", and then
      get rid of special handling of sockets. As a side effect of this patch,
      "socket:[family=\$:type=\$:protocol=\$]" in the policy files has to be
      rewritten to "socket:[\$]".

Version 0.2.5   2019/12/25   Bug fix release.

Fix 2020/04/09

    @ Fix wrong put_page() usage in cs_dump_page().

      cs_dump_page() for 5.6+ was by error using wrong function to put page.

Version 0.2.6   2020/04/13   Bug fix release.

Fix 2020/08/19

    @ Fix wrong cs_search_binary_handler() mapping.

      When support for 5.8 kernel was added, cs_search_binary_handler() for
      3.7- was by error mapped to wrong function.

Version 0.2.7   2020/08/20   Bug fix release.

Fix 2020/10/24

    @ Fix /proc pathname calculation for Linux 5.8+ kernels.

      ccs_realpath() for 5.8+ was by error not using proc_pid_ns() when
      calculating /proc pathname.

Version 0.2.8   2020/11/11   Bug fix release.

Fix 2021/03/13

    @ Fix cs_kernel_service().

      Kernels from 5.5 to 5.11 are using PF_KTHREAD flag for the io_uring
      worker threads.

Fix 2021/03/15

    @ Skip permission checks for fileless execution requests.

      Kernels from 4.18 to 5.8 are using call_usermodehelper_setup_file() for
      starting program without a valid pathname on a filesystem.
      /sbin/modprobe from dockerd process could not load bpfilter.ko module
      because cs_start_execve() cannot calculate pathname of program without
      a valid pathname. Thus, allow call_usermodehelper_setup_file() to bypass
      permission checks and suppress domain transitions.

Version 0.2.9   2021/04/01   Bug fix release.

Fix 2021/05/31

    @ Use killable wait than interruptible wait.

      It is inconsistent that waiting for a lock is interruptible while
      memory allocation with that lock held is not interruptible.

      Replace interruptible wait with killable wait, for it is not desirable
      to terminate a process when that process was doing a domain transition
      via "transition=" keyword.

    @ Extend availability of "transition=" keyword to socket operations.

      Policies for daemons can be split into a "setup" phase and a "runtime"
      phase, distinguished by when they invoke `listen(2)` on their service
      socket. It would be extremely useful to be able to trigger a domain
      transition when that happens, so that the program can have its sandbox
      tightened when its setup phase is over.

Version 0.2.10   2021/06/06   Functionality enhancement release.

Fix 2023/05/21

    @ Fix incomplete execve() failure handling in LKM-based LSM version.

      When cs_start_execve() failed before CS_TASK_IS_IN_EXECVE is set,
      cs_clear_execve(-1, ptr) from cs_find_task_security() from
      cs_cred_prepare() was not called. As a result, retried execve()
      request did not check execute permission by calling
      cs_start_execve() from cs_bprm_check_security(). Change
      cs_start_execve() to clear "struct cs_security"->cs_request_info
      before returning an error.

      Also, commit 84029fd04c20 ("memcg: account security cred as well to
      kmemcg") changed security_prepare_creds() to use GFP_KERNEL_ACCOUNT.
      As a result, cs_clear_execve(-1, ptr) from cs_find_task_security()
      from cs_cred_prepare() was not called. Since GFP_KERNEL_ACCOUNT is
      defined as GFP_KERNEL | __GFP_ACCOUNT, change cs_cred_prepare() to
      check whether gfp flag contains GFP_KERNEL.

Version 0.2.11   2023/05/27   Security bug fix release.

Fix 2024/03/31

    @ Fix a UAF bug introduced by an oversight in TOMOYO revision 2930.

      Backport commit 2f03fc340cac ("tomoyo: fix UAF write bug in
      tomoyo_write_control()").

Version 0.2.12   2024/04/01   Security bug fix release.

Fix 2024/06/28

    @ Unblock move_mount() system call.

      Since util-linux 2.39 started using libmount-mountfd-support,
      implementing appropriate permission check for move_mount() became
      necessary for successfully booting a Linux system.

Version 0.2.13   2024/07/15   Bug fix release.

Fix 2024/11/11

    @ Require CONFIG_KALLSYMS_ALL=y for building LKM-based LSM modules on 6.12+ kernels.

      Since it became impossible to guess the address of static_calls_table
      variable in Linux 6.12, I had to depend on CONFIG_KALLSYMS_ALL=y for
      building LKM-based LSM modules with 6.12+ kernels. Also, I updated to
      utilize CONFIG_KALLSYMS_ALL=y for building LKM-based LSM modules with
      earlier kernels if CONFIG_KALLSYMS_ALL is defined.

Version 0.2.14   2024/11/11   Bug fix release.

Fix 2024/11/27

    @ Fix probing of security_ops with CONFIG_KALLSYMS_ALL=y case.

      There was a bug when loading LKM-based LSM modules on CentOS 7.

Version 0.2.15   2024/11/28   Bug fix release.

Fix 2026/09/28

    @ Fix mkdir() mode value check.

      Permission check for mkdir operation was by error using raw mode value
      rather than value masked with S_IALLUGO.

Version 0.2.16   2026/09/28   Bug fix release.
